.SH "NAME"
(*econf\&.File)\&.Close \- release the memory backing an econf\&.File object

.SH "SYNOPSIS"

.sp
.ft B
.nf
import "github\&.com/openSUSE/libeconf/go/econf"
.fi
.ft
.sp
.nf
\fBfunc (f *econf\&.File) Close() error\fR
.fi

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

.PP
\fB(*File)\&.Close\fR is a dummy method with no effect\&. It exists solely so that code written
against
\fI*File\fR
compiles and behaves correctly without any changes, regardless of whether
it is linked against
\fIlibeconf Go Bindings\fR
(the cgo\-based package, where
\fBClose\fR
releases C memory; see
\fBeconf_go_File_Close\fR(3)
in that package's own manual) or
\fIlibeconf native Go\fR
(this package, which holds no C memory to release)\&.

.PP
It is safe to call
\fBClose\fR
multiple times, and on a
\fInil\fR
\fI*File\fR\&.
\fIf\fR
must not be used again after
\fBClose\fR
has been called\&.

.PP
A finalizer is registered on every
\fI*File\fR
returned by this package, so the underlying memory is eventually released
even if
\fBClose\fR
is never called explicitly\&. Callers should nonetheless call
\fBClose\fR
deterministically (typically via
\fCdefer f\&.Close()\fR) rather than relying on the garbage collector\&.

.SH "RETURN VALUE"

.PP
\fBClose\fR
always returns
\fInil\fR\&. The
\fIerror\fR
return value exists so that
\fI*File\fR
satisfies
\fBio\&.Closer\fR\&.

.SH "ERRORS"

.PP
This function does not return an error\&.

.SH "EXAMPLE"
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 1
.\}
.nf

f, err := econf\&.ReadFile("/etc/example\&.conf", "=", "#")
if err != nil {
    log\&.Fatal(err)
}
defer f\&.Close()

.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp

